{"chapter": "categories", "node_kind": "remark", "parent_tag": "003O", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$\\mathcal{A}$", "MATH_0002": "$\\mathcal{B}$", "MATH_0003": "$\\mathcal{C}$", "MATH_0004": "$F : \\mathcal{A} \\to \\mathcal{C}$", "MATH_0005": "$G : \\mathcal{B} \\to \\mathcal{C}$", "MATH_0006": "$2$", "MATH_0007": "$\\mathcal{A} \\times_\\mathcal{C} \\mathcal{B}$", "MATH_0008": "$(A, B, C, a, b)$", "MATH_0009": "$A, B, C$", "MATH_0010": "$\\mathcal{A}, \\mathcal{B}, \\mathcal{C}$", "MATH_0011": "$a : F(A) \\to C$", "MATH_0012": "$b : G(B) \\to C$", "MATH_0013": "$(A, B, C, a, b) \\to (A', B', C', a', b')$", "MATH_0014": "$A \\to A', B \\to B', C \\to C'$", "MATH_0015": "$a, b, a', b'$", "MATH_0016": "$2$", "MATH_0017": "$(A, B, C, a, b) \\mapsto (A, B, b^{-1} \\circ a)$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{example-2-fibre-product-categories}"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{remark}\n\\label{remark-other-description-2-fibre-product}\n", "suffix": "\n\\end{remark}\n\n"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:52c06317c6c958841f02346b239adde383de14a95c99929d3573d8df0de8533c", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:e36150d1a5f29f3b8bfca8c0a5bcffff0b38353ce6b8fc21ccfe5d64b5e96dbc", "source_text": "Let <MATH_0001>, <MATH_0002>, and <MATH_0003> be categories.\nLet <MATH_0004> and <MATH_0005>\nbe functors. Another, slightly more symmetrical, construction of a <MATH_0006>-fibre\nproduct <MATH_0007> is as follows.\nAn object is a quintuple <MATH_0008> where <MATH_0009> are objects\nof <MATH_0010> and where <MATH_0011>\nand <MATH_0012> are isomorphisms. A morphism\n<MATH_0013> is given by a triple\nof morphisms <MATH_0014> compatible with the morphisms\n<MATH_0015>. We can prove directly that this leads to a <MATH_0016>-fibre\nproduct. However, it is easier to observe that the functor\n<MATH_0017> gives an equivalence\nfrom the category of quintuples to the category constructed